EXPERIMENT TASK — CONSTRUCT 2WIKI QUESTION GRAPH

We are constructing a rich grounded RDF graph for one 2WikiMultiHopQA question.

The CURRENT SOURCE is the extraction target.

The question, expected answer, and benchmark evidence relations are coordination
context. They identify relations that the final graph must not miss, but they
do not define the complete graph and they are not textual evidence.

For the CURRENT SOURCE:

1. MANDATORY BENCHMARK COVERAGE

Represent every benchmark evidence relation that is explicitly supported by
CURRENT SOURCE.

These relations are a required minimum. They must not be omitted.

2. BROAD SOURCE EXTRACTION

After ensuring the mandatory benchmark relations are represented, continue
through the entire CURRENT SOURCE and extract all other useful explicit atomic
facts that can be grounded correctly.

Do not stop after extracting the benchmark relation.

Do not restrict extraction to facts needed for the question or expected answer.

Treat each sentence or local clause independently: when it contains a useful
explicit factual relation, represent that relation unless doing so would violate
the grounding or graph-quality rules.

The benchmark evidence tells you what must not be missed.
It is not the extraction target and is not a reason to stop.

Reuse semantic names already present in the accepted graph when they denote the
same thing.

Every returned subject, predicate, and object must cite exact evidence from the
CURRENT SOURCE.

Do not use the question, expected answer, benchmark evidence relations, accepted
graph, or another source as textual evidence.

Python owns source identity; never return source IDs.

Question: Where was the composer of song Theater (Song) born?

Expected answer: Munich

Benchmark evidence relations:
1. Theater | composer | Ralph Siegel
2. Ralph Siegel | place of birth | Munich

Accepted semantic graph so far:
(empty)

CURRENT SOURCE — 2Wiki paragraph 3

{
  "title": "Ralph Siegel",
  "text": "Ralph Siegel (born 30 September 1945 in Munich) is a German record producer and songwriter. He is married to the opera singer Kriemhild Jahn. He has three daughters, one of them Giulia Siegel divorced from German entrepreneur Hans Wehrmann. Siegel is one of the most notable figures at the Eurovision Song Contest, in which he has participated with 24 songs so far, the latest being the 2017 Sammarinese entry \"Spirit of the Night\" by Valentina Monetta and Jimmie Wilson. In 1982, Siegel's and Bernd Meinunger's song \"Ein bißchen Frieden\" (A Little Peace), performed by Nicole won the Contest and became a hit in Europe. In February 2010, it was announced by RTÉ that he would have an entry in the Irish National Final-Eurosong 2010. The song titled 'River of Silence' was performed by Lee Bradshaw. It finished in last place. He also composed 'C'était ma vie', performed by Lys Assia for representing Switzerland in the Eurovision Song Contest. She reached an 8th position in Swiss National Final. Siegel organized a comeback event for the Disco band Dschinghis Khan in June 2018. Dschinghis Khan had been organized by Stiegel and Dr. Bernd Meinunger to compete in the 1979 Eurovision Song Contest. After the success of their first hit, Dschinghis Khan, both men continued to write popular songs for this band, including Moskau and Hadschi Halef Omar. Siegel makes a rare appearance playing classical guitar in 1984 video footage of Dschinghis Khan performing Corrida and Ole Ole."
}

